.TH EUCLID-CLI-ETS-START-SERVER 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ets-start-server \- ask the manager to run a transfer server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ets start-server
.BR \-\-server\-id | \-n
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Marks a transfer server as one that should be running.
.PP
The command returns as soon as that intent is recorded, not once the
server is up. The Euclid manager reconciles the defined servers against
the processes it runs every few seconds, and spawns the
.BR euclid-ftp (8)
or
.BR euclid-sftp (8)
process on its next pass. Use
.BR euclid-cli-ets-list-servers (1)
to watch the observed
.I state
catch up with the
.IR desiredState .
.PP
Because this records a state rather than sending a command, it is safe
to repeat: starting an already-running server changes nothing. The state
is also durable, so a manager restart brings every server marked running
back by itself.
.PP
If the server does not come up, the reason is in the log of the spawned
process rather than in this command's output - the usual causes are a
port already in use, a missing or unreadable SSH host key, or an
unwritable spool directory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-server\-id " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the server to start. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The desired state was recorded; the server's definition is printed as
JSON with \fIdesiredState\fR \fIRUNNING\fR. This does
.I not
mean the server is listening yet.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, the caller is not an authenticated
administrator, the server could not be reached, or no server has that ID
(HTTP 404).
.SH EXAMPLES
Start a server and confirm it came up:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ets start-server \e
    --server-id sftp1

sleep 10
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ets get-server \e
    --server-id sftp1
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ets (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-stop-server (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-list-servers (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-create-server (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jens Vogt <jens.vogt@opitz-consulting.com>
